[38;5;231m⏺[39m Confirmed the wiring while I was in there: [38;5;153mindex.html:13[39m loads [38;5;153mapp.js[39m as a plain [38;5;153m<script>[39m (no [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m), while
  [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m are CommonJS for Node. Two disjoint module worlds, no bundler, no test runner, no
  dependencies. A "shared across the app" module has to pick a side of that — I'll come back to it as the next question.

  First, persistence. The fork: how long the stored userId outlives the login that produced it, which also decides how
  wrong your tracking can get on a shared device.

  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[0m — survives reloads and navigation within the tab, dies when the tab closes. Cheap, and a stale id
    can't be attributed to the next person at the machine. Costs: a second tab starts with no id until that tab logs in.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m — survives browser restarts, shared across tabs. Matches "persist" most literally and is what you'd
    want for cross-tab consistency. Costs: an id left behind by the previous user gets attached to the next person's
    actions unless logout explicitly clears it, and you now owe a clear-on-logout path.
  - [1mIn-memory only[0m — a module-level variable, gone on reload. Simplest and safest, but doesn't satisfy "persist."

  Both storage options are cheap to swap later [3mif[0m reads and writes go through one session module rather than touching
  [38;5;153mStorage[39m directly — which is the main thing the design should lock in. My recommendation is [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m: it
  satisfies "persists across the app" for the session it describes, without inheriting the stale-attribution problem
  that makes [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m tracking data quietly untrustworthy.
